IPCC Atlas plots for Central Asia
=================================

Python scripts for the maps of observed trends and projected changes over Central Asia
(45-90 E, 30-56 N) in B. Fallah, "Climate Change in Central Asia and Expected Impacts",
in Towards Sustainable Future of Central Asia, Advances in Global Change Research 84,
Springer, doi:10.1007/978-3-032-29989-5_2.

All data come from the IPCC AR6 WGI Interactive Atlas (https://interactive-atlas.ipcc.ch)
or from the dataset behind it on the Copernicus Climate Data Store,
"Gridded monthly climate projection dataset underpinning the IPCC AR6 Interactive Atlas",
doi:10.24381/cds.5292a2b0 (CC BY 4.0).


Snowfall at global warming levels (book Fig. 2.8)
-------------------------------------------------

Fig. 2.8 shows the change in annual mean snowfall (mm per day, water equivalent) at
+2 degC (SSP2-4.5, 29 models), +3 degC (SSP3-7.0, 23 models) and +4 degC (SSP5-8.5,
18 models) relative to 1981-2010, CMIP6 multi-model mean. Dots mark grid cells without
a robust signal: fewer than 70 % of the models show a change larger than internal
variability (threshold sqrt(2) * 1.645 * sd / sqrt(20), sd the interannual standard
deviation over the baseline, as in the IPCC AR6 advanced method). This rule reproduces
the dots of the printed figure best (robustness threshold 0.7 in snow.py).

Reproduce it from scratch in three steps (Python 3.10 or newer):

  conda create -n atlas -c conda-forge python=3.12 xarray netcdf4 dask pandas \
        matplotlib cartopy regionmask cdsapi
  conda activate atlas

  1. Download CMIP6 snowfall (about 5 GB). Needs a free CDS account, the dataset licence
     accepted on its web page, and the API key in ~/.cdsapirc:
         python download_atlas_snowfall.py data
  2. Get the official warming-level table and compute the changes:
         curl -LO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/IPCC-WG1/Atlas/main/warming-levels/CMIP6_Atlas_WarmingLevels.csv
         python atlas_snowfall_gwl.py data CMIP6_Atlas_WarmingLevels.csv derived
  3. Plot:
         python plot_snowfall_gwl.py derived figures

Outputs:
  derived/prsn_gwl{2,3,4}_<scenario>_base<period>.nc
      delta (per model), delta_mean, baseline_mean, rel_mean (percent), robust
      (simple method, 1 = more than 80 % sign agreement), advanced (1 robust signal,
      2 no change or no robust signal, 3 conflicting signals), central_year
  derived/country_means_per_model.csv
      area-weighted country means for Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan, Uzbekistan
      and Turkmenistan, one row per model
  figures/snowfall_gwl_central_asia.pdf/.png
      absolute and relative change, robustness dots, country means
  figures/snowfall_gwl_book_style_check.pdf/.png
      the printed colour scale for the 1981-2010 and 1995-2014 baselines

Method (as in github.com/IPCC-WG1/Atlas): each model's change is its mean over the
20-year window [n-9, n+10] around the year n in which it reaches the warming level
(relative to 1850-1900) minus its baseline mean; maps show the multi-model mean.

Note: the CDS copy of the CMIP6 SSP3-7.0 snowfall file (product v2.0-final) holds
monthly totals although its metadata say daily means. atlas_snowfall_gwl.py compares
every scenario with the historical run and divides by the number of days in the month
when the ratio shows monthly totals.


Other scripts
-------------

  pr.py               precipitation trend 1980-2015 with significance (pr/map.nc, pr/pvalue.nc)
  plot_figur_temp.py  temperature trend 1980-2015 with significance (tas/map.nc, tas/pvalue.nc)
  snow.py             the original Fig. 2.8 plot from Interactive Atlas downloads
                      (snow_2/, snow_3/, snow_4/ with map.nc and robustness.nc)
  central_asia.py     topography map from ETOPO1


Citation for the data
---------------------

Gutierrez, J.M., et al., 2021: Atlas. In: Climate Change 2021: The Physical Science
Basis. Contribution of Working Group I to the Sixth Assessment Report of the IPCC.
Cambridge University Press, 1927-2058, doi:10.1017/9781009157896.021.
Interactive Atlas available from http://interactive-atlas.ipcc.ch

Iturbide, M., et al., 2022: Implementation of FAIR principles in the IPCC: the WGI AR6
Atlas repository. Scientific Data 9, 629, doi:10.1038/s41597-022-01739-y.


License: MIT (code). Data: CC BY 4.0 (IPCC Atlas dataset).


Snow water equivalent at the same warming levels (CMIP6, ESGF)
--------------------------------------------------------------

The IPCC Atlas has no snow water equivalent (SWE). These scripts take CMIP6 monthly SWE
(snw, table LImon) from ESGF for every model of the Atlas warming-level table, cut out
Central Asia over OPeNDAP, and apply the same warming-level windows and 1981-2010 baseline:

  python fetch_esgf_snw.py CMIP6_Atlas_WarmingLevels.csv esgf
  curl ... ERA5 geopotential (any one time) for the elevation masks, see swe_gwl.py
  python swe_gwl.py esgf CMIP6_Atlas_WarmingLevels.csv era5_orography.nc derived swe_out
  python swe_figure.py esgf CMIP6_Atlas_WarmingLevels.csv derived figures

swe_gwl.py writes the change in annual mean SWE, peak SWE (annual maximum of monthly SWE)
and snowfall for the mountains of the five Central Asian countries (above 1500 m and
2000 m) and for elevations below 2000 m. Cells with snow all year (glaciers in the models)
are left out. swe_figure.py maps the multi-model median change in annual mean SWE and shows
Kazakhstan means of snowfall, annual mean SWE and peak SWE for every model.
